samples.evidence:
```ts
try {
  const open = (await import('open')).default;
  await open(oauthUrl);
  opened = true;
} catch { /* fall through and print the URL */ }
if (!opened) { console.error('Could not open a browser. ...'); console.error(oauthUrl); }
```
`open` v11 (packages/cli/package.json:28) resolves as soon as the child process is *spawned* when `wait` is not set — it does not report whether a browser actually launched. On a headless box, SSH session, container or CI runner, `xdg-open` usually exists and exits non-zero (or does nothing) asynchronously, so `open()` resolves, `opened` becomes `true`, and the URL is never printed. The previous code printed the URL unconditionally before attempting `open`. Net effect: `wafflebase login` prints only "Opening browser for GitHub login...", hangs for the 30 s callback timeout, and gives the user no way to complete the flow. Related: without a `wait` option, `open` does not attach an `error` listener to the subprocess, so a spawn `ENOENT` is emitted asynchronously on the ChildProcess *after* `open()` already resolved — it escapes the `try/catch` entirely and surfaces as an uncaught exception rather than the `!opened` fallback.

```ts
if (cliState) {
  const port = cliState.port;
  if (port < 1024 || port > 65535) { throw new BadRequestException('Invalid CLI port'); }
```
`cliState` can only come from `this.cliAuthStore.consumeState(...)` (auth.controller.ts:191), and the only producer of CLI state entries is `GitHubAuthGuard.canActivate`, which stores a state only inside `if (Number.isInteger(portNum) && portNum >= 1024 && portNum <= 65535)` (github-auth.guard.ts:87). No other call site of `createState` exists in `src/` (grep for `createState` finds only the guard and specs). The branch is therefore unreachable in production. Not harmful, but it hides the fact that a CLI request with an out-of-range port now silently falls through to `startWebState` (github-auth.guard.ts:115) and completes a *browser* login — session cookies + redirect to `FRONTEND_URL` — while the CLI that asked for it waits out its 30 s timeout with no error at all.

```ts
res.cookie?.(OAUTH_STATE_COOKIE, value, { ... });
return `${WEB_STATE_PREFIX}${value}`;
```
The optional call means that if `res.cookie` is unavailable for any reason, the guard still returns a `w.`-prefixed state and forwards the user to GitHub. The callback then reaches `verifyCallbackState`, finds `req.cookies?.[OAUTH_STATE_COOKIE]` undefined, and throws `UnauthorizedException` (auth.controller.ts:182) — i.e. every browser login fails with a message that blames the user ("Login session expired or invalid") and no server-side signal about the real cause. A missing `res.cookie` is a programming error, not a condition to swallow; the guard should fail loudly rather than mint an unverifiable state.

```ts
if (!res.ok || !res.bytes) {
  io.stderr(backendErrorEnvelope(res.data, { code: 'HTTP_ERROR', message: `HTTP ${res.status}` }, command));
```
The condition also fires when `res.ok` is true but `bytes` is missing. In that case `res.data` is null (http-client.ts:184 parses JSON or yields null), so the emitted envelope is `{"error":{"code":"HTTP_ERROR","message":"HTTP 200",...}}` — a self-contradictory message that tells an agent nothing about the actual failure (empty body / missing bytes). The pre-change code printed `{ error: { code: 'HTTP_ERROR' } }` with no message, so this is not a regression, but the new message is actively misleading for that branch.

```ts
let acting: Command | undefined;
program.hook('preAction', (_thisCommand, actionCommand) => { acting = actionCommand; });
```
The hook is attached to the program passed in, which the signature explicitly allows a caller to supply (`program: Command = buildProgram()`). Calling `runCli(sameProgram, ...)` twice — which the test suite does with injected programs — accumulates one hook per call on the same `Command`, all of which then run on every subsequent parse. Separately, `acting` is never reset, so any throw that escapes after an action has completed (or a `CommanderError` raised while processing a *later* argument) is attributed to whichever subcommand last started, rather than being omitted as the doc comment promises ("omitted rather than guessed when no command is known").

